<BaseLayout
  title="Research"
  description="CAV research methods: home-language study, refusal of false equivalents, and community authority over publication."
>
  <PageHeader
    kicker="Methods"
    title="Study the remainder."
    lede="Research at CAV begins where a convenient translation starts to lie. This page is a methods stub — a public account of how we intend to work, not a claim that the work is finished."
  />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-12 sm:px-8">
    <article class="measure prose-cav text-[1.05rem]">
      <h2>What counts as an anomalous virtue</h2>
      <p>
        A candidate concept is specific, ethically loaded, and poorly served by the
        portable English of values training. It may be a virtue word, a practice, or a
        moral emotion. It is anomalous not because it is exotic, but because it does
        not sit inside the usual set — respect, kindness, dignity, tolerance — without
        remainder.
      </p>

      <h2>Working method</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Attest, don’t harvest.</strong> We record how a term is used by
          speakers and practitioners before we interpret it. Unpublished or restricted
          knowledge stays unpublished.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep competing accounts.</strong> Disagreement inside a language is
          data. A single “authentic” gloss is usually a political act pretending to be
          a dictionary.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Mark the seam.</strong> Every entry states what English almost says,
          then what it cannot. That seam is the object of study.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Separate illustration from claim.</strong> Early catalog notes are
          labeled as examples. Formal papers will carry sources. We will not invent
          citations to look finished.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Return draft to source.</strong> Communities named in an entry review
          language, withhold what they choose, and may withdraw cooperation.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <p>
        It is not a listicle of untranslatable words, not a ranking of cultures, and
        not a toolkit for performing sensitivity. Comparative work will be slow,
        cited, and revisable.
      </p>
      <p>
        A fuller methods paper — field protocols, review standards, and catalog
        taxonomy — will be published here as it is written. Until then, treat this
        page as the hypothesis we are willing to be corrected on.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
